ve lifestyle intervention in MASLD due to their potential ability to reduce circulating glucose spikes, insulin resistance, hepatic glucose influx, and de novo lipogenesis [11]. || High-fibre diets, how ever, are not always associated with LGI, as some fibre types can have a lesser impact on postprandial glycemia and hyperinsulinemia [14].","Original article The effect of a two-w eek low glycaemic index, higher fibre diet versus high glycaemic index diet on body composition, ectopic lipids, inflammatory biomarkers, gastrointestinal hormones and gut microbiota in metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD): A pilot randomized cross-over study Amina A. Al-Aw adi a,b,c,d,1 , Jane I. Grove a,b , Stephen Bawden b,e,f,g , Amrita Vijay b,h , Ana M. Valdes b,h , Penny Gow land b,e , Moir a A. Taylor b,i,2 , Guruprasad P. Aithal a,b,*,2 a Nottingham Digestive Diseases Centre, Translational Medical Sciences, School of Medicine, University of Nottingham, Nottingham, UK b National Institute for Health Research (NIHR) No",ok,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,PIIS240545772600402X.pdf,1.000,10,"Within-group compari- sons show ed a significant reduction in HOMA-IR after LGI diet (p = 0.043). || A significant difference in beta-diversity change w as found between the two diets (LGI = ␀ 0.02 vs HGI = +0.0001, p = 0.014). || For inflammatory markers, a significant decrease in IL-6 w as observed following the LGI diet (␀ 0.9 pg/mL, p = 0.028, r = 0.45). || Both diets resulted in a slight increase in the ATP forward rate, though the changes were not statistically significant (HGI = 0.3, p = 0.53; || Although a greater numerical reduction in IL-6 levels w as seen after the HGI diet (␀ 1.13 pg/mL), this w as not statistically signifi- cant (p = 0.61). || Both diets resulted in a slight reduction in alpha- diversity, with a significant decrease observed after the HGI diet (␀ 0.26, p = 0.03) (Appendix S2 Table S5). || Absolute level of liver fat w as reduced more after LGI diet (␀ 1.56 ± 3.16%) compared to the HGI diet (␀ 1.03 ± 5.09%), although this difference w as not statistically significant (p = 0.4, r = -0.26). || For gut hormones, no significant change w as observed after the HGI (␀ 4.6 pg/mL, p = 0.23), while a significant reduction in glucagon levels w as found following the LGI diet (␀ 13.72 pg/mL, p = 0.028).","A significant difference in beta-diversity change w as found between the two diets (LGI = ␀ 0.02 vs HGI = +0.0001, p = 0.014). || Both diets resulted in a slight increase in the ATP forward rate, though the changes were not statistically significant (HGI = 0.3, p = 0.53; || Absolute level of liver fat w as reduced more after LGI diet (␀ 1.56 ± 3.16%) compared to the HGI diet (␀ 1.03 ± 5.09%), although this difference w as not statistically significant (p = 0.4, r = -0.26). || Relative liver fat fraction w as mar kedly reduced following the LGI diet (8.57%).Observ ations in the LGI group coincided with changes in blood glycaemic biomarkers, including a significant reduction in HOMA-IR (␀ 1.78, p = 0.043). || Additionally, the LGI diet resulted in significant reductions in body weight (␀ 1.2 kg, p = 0.018) and body mass index (␀ 0.38 kg/ m 2 , p = 0.017), as well as increased pre-meal appetite scores during the middle of the diet period and decreased interleukin 6; || A significant difference between the two diets w as observed in changes in body weight (p = 0.018, r = -0.89) and BMI (p = 0.043, r = 0.90), with significant reductions following the LGI diet only (Body weight: ␀ 1.2 ± 1.06 kg, and BMI: ␀ 0.38 ± 0.35, both p < 0.05). || Both diets resulted in a slight reduction in alpha- diversity, with a significant decrease observed after the HGI diet (␀ 0.26, p = 0.03) (Appendix S2 Table S5). || Acetic acid significantly decreased after HGI (p = 0.028) and show ed a non- significant increase after LGI (p = 0.31), also yielding a large ef- fect (r = 0.70).","A significant difference between the two diets w as observed in changes in body weight (p = 0.018, r = -0.89) and BMI (p = 0.043, r = 0.90), with significant reductions following the LGI diet only (Body weight: ␀ 1.2 ± 1.06 kg, and BMI: ␀ 0.38 ± 0.35, both p < 0.05). || Additionally, the LGI diet resulted in significant reductions in body weight (␀ 1.2 kg, p = 0.018) and body mass index (␀ 0.38 kg/ m 2 , p = 0.017), as well as increased pre-meal appetite scores during the middle of the diet period and decreased interleukin 6; || Additionally, studies indicate that liver fat reduction can occur without significant changes in body weight or BMI in pa- tients with MASLD or metabolic syndrome [45–47]. || Fractional change from baseline show ed a significant mean reduction in body weight of 1.22% (95%CI: ␀ 2.19% to ␀ 0.26%) and BMI of 1.17% (95%CI: ␀ 2.07% to ␀ 0.27%) following the LGI diet, compared to the HGI diet, as illustrated in Fig. || Within-group compari- sons show ed a significant reduction in HOMA-IR after LGI diet (p = 0.043). || A significant difference in beta-diversity change w as found between the two diets (LGI = ␀ 0.02 vs HGI = +0.0001, p = 0.014). || For inflammatory markers, a significant decrease in IL-6 w as observed following the LGI diet (␀ 0.9 pg/mL, p = 0.028, r = 0.45). || Both diets resulted in a slight increase in the ATP forward rate, though the changes were not statistically significant (HGI = 0.3, p = 0.53;","Within-group compari- sons show ed a significant reduction in HOMA-IR after LGI diet (p = 0.043). || Relative liver fat fraction w as mar kedly reduced following the LGI diet (8.57%).Observ ations in the LGI group coincided with changes in blood glycaemic biomarkers, including a significant reduction in HOMA-IR (␀ 1.78, p = 0.043). || How ever, changes in gly- caemic biomarkers show ed greater reductions following the LGI diet compare d to the HGI diet in fasting glucose (␀ 0.84 vs ␀ 0.35 mmol/L, r = 0.45), insulin (␀ 2.94 vs + 0.3 mIU/L, r = 0.51) and HOMA-IR (␀ 1.78 vs ␀ 0.29, r = 0.51). || A significant difference in beta-diversity change w as found between the two diets (LGI = ␀ 0.02 vs HGI = +0.0001, p = 0.014). || For inflammatory markers, a significant decrease in IL-6 w as observed following the LGI diet (␀ 0.9 pg/mL, p = 0.028, r = 0.45). || Both diets resulted in a slight increase in the ATP forward rate, though the changes were not statistically significant (HGI = 0.3, p = 0.53; || Although a greater numerical reduction in IL-6 levels w as seen after the HGI diet (␀ 1.13 pg/mL), this w as not statistically signifi- cant (p = 0.61). || Both diets resulted in a slight reduction in alpha- diversity, with a significant decrease observed after the HGI diet (␀ 0.26, p = 0.03) (Appendix S2 Table S5).","A meta - analysis of 64 RCTs on healthy subjects also confirmed that di- etary fibre interventions specifically increase Bifidobacterium and Lactobacillus levels without affecting α-diversity [53] similar to the present study. || Within-group compari- sons show ed a significant reduction in HOMA-IR after LGI diet (p = 0.043). || A significant difference in beta-diversity change w as found between the two diets (LGI = ␀ 0.02 vs HGI = +0.0001, p = 0.014). || For inflammatory markers, a significant decrease in IL-6 w as observed following the LGI diet (␀ 0.9 pg/mL, p = 0.028, r = 0.45). || Both diets resulted in a slight increase in the ATP forward rate, though the changes were not statistically significant (HGI = 0.3, p = 0.53; || Although a greater numerical reduction in IL-6 levels w as seen after the HGI diet (␀ 1.13 pg/mL), this w as not statistically signifi- cant (p = 0.61). || Both diets resulted in a slight reduction in alpha- diversity, with a significant decrease observed after the HGI diet (␀ 0.26, p = 0.03) (Appendix S2 Table S5). || Acetic acid significantly decreased after HGI (p = 0.028) and show ed a non- significant increase after LGI (p = 0.31), also yielding a large ef- fect (r = 0.70).","Within-group compari- sons show ed a significant reduction in HOM