he development of several preventio; 10%; changes in gut microbiota is an important part of elucidating the mechanism of action; increase with polyphenol consumption [16]; changes in gut microbiota; 0.24%; 0.54%; 30 kg/m; 25%; 32%","Nutrition & Metabolism (2024) 21:83 https://doi.org/10.1186/s12986-024-00851-7 RESEARCH Effect of Sinetrol® Xpur on metabolic health and adiposity by interactions with gut microbiota: a randomized, open label, dose– response clinical trial Jananee Muralidharan1, Cindy Romain1, Linda Chung2, Pedro Alcaraz2, Francisco Javier Martínez‑Noguera2, Mayoura Keophiph || Methods In this open label study, we evaluated the effect of Sinetrol® Xpur supplementation on healthy but over‑ weight/obese adults (20–50 yrs) for 16 weeks. || Results We observed HDL, HbA1C, LDL and leptin improved significantly over 16 weeks, irrespective of the dosage. || Conclusions Overall chronic supplementation of Sinetrol® Xpur, irrespective of their dose improved HDL, HbA1c, LDL and leptin and tended to decrease visceral adipose tissue via changes in gut microbiota. || Keywords Obesity, Polyphenols, Gut microbiota, Fat loss, Metabolism Open Access © The Author(s) 2024. || Fla - vanones such as naringenin and hesperidin have shown glucose lowering and lipid modulating effects in invitro studies, however the human studies have shown incon - sistent results [10, 11].","Muralidharan et al. Nutrition & Metabolism (2024) 21:83 https://doi.org/10.1186/s12986-024-00851-7 RESEARCH Effect of Sinetrol® Xpur on metabolic health and adiposity by interactions with gut microbiota: a randomized, open label, dose– response clinical trial Jananee Muralidharan1, Cindy Romain1, Linda Chung2, Pedro Alcaraz2, Francisco Javier Martínez‑Noguera2, Mayoura Keophiphath3, Benjamin Lelouvier4, Patricia Ancel5, Benedicte Gaborit5 and Julien Cases1* Abstract Background Sinetrol ® Xpur is a polyphenolic ingredient rich in citrus flavonoids that has shown weight loss effects in previous studies. The dose dependent nature, gut microbial actions of this product has not been explored prev",ok,,Lacticaseibacillus paracasei K56; lactobacillus and; Bifidobacterium are; Lactobacillus gasseri SBT2055; Lactobacillus plantarum; Bifidobacterium longum; lactobacillus gas,SBT2055; BNR17,107 CFU/day; 109 CFU/day; 1010 CFU/day; 1011 CFU/ day; 109 CFU; 10 11 CFU/ day; 108 CFU; 107 CFU; 106 CFU/day,60 days; 12 weeks; 3 months; 60 day,74 subjects; 66 subjects; n = 13; n = 14; n = 12,pilot; randomized; placebo controlled; clinical trial,capsules; capsule; diet; exercise; milk,p = 0.004; p = 0.0007; p = 0.018; p = 0.003; p = 0.002; p = 0.01; p < 0.05; p < 0.01; p < 0.001; p > 0.05; p = 0.035,"30 kg/m2; 25%; 30%; decreases in percent body fat (p = 0; increases were observed in the placebo and VH_K56 groups compared with baseline values; changes in body fat mass, visceral fat area, weight, body mass index, waist circumference and hi; changes were made; increased prevalence of obesity has reached epidemic proportions, and presents a critical public h; increased mortality from type 2 diabetes (T2D), hypertension, and cardiovascular diseases, as well; changes in the gut microbial composition and function contribute to the pathophysi - ology of ob; reduces body weight gain, improves glucose tolerance in rodents through anti-inflammatory effect; reduced fat mass and serum lipid profile concurrently with down - regulation of lipogenic gene e; reduced abdomi - nal adiposity, waist and hip circumference or improved glucolipid metabolism fo; reduced body and fat mass and improved lipid metabolism [23]; improvement in the past two months; changes of dietary habits and the daily walking step numbers recorded by motion recorder was use; changes of physical activity; changes in body fat percent- age (PBF) and visceral fat area (VFA) from baseline to day 60","Nutrition & Metabolism (2023) 20:16 https://doi.org/10.1186/s12986-023-00739-y RESEARCH Effect of different doses of Lacticaseibacillus paracasei K56 on body fat and metabolic parameters in adult individuals with obesity: a pilot study Guzailinuer Kadeer1, Wanrui Fu1, Yaqi He1, Ying Feng1*, Wei‑Hsein Liu2, Wei‑Lian Hung2*, Haotian Feng2 and Wen Zhao2 Abstract Background Studies || The purpose of this study was to evaluate the effect of a novel probiotic strain Lacticaseibacillus paracasei K56 on body fat and metabolic biomarkers in adult individuals with obesity. || Methods 74 adult subjects with obesity (body mass index ≥ 30 kg/m2, or percent body fat > 25% for men, percent body fat > 30% for women) were randomized into 5 groups and supplemented with different doses of K56 (groups VL_K56, L_K56, H_K56, and VH_K56: K56 capsules, 2 × 107 CFU/day, 2 × 109 CFU/day, 2 × 1010 CFU/day, 2 × 1011 CFU/ day, respectively) or placebo (group Pla: plac || Results The results showed that the L_K56 group had significant decreases in percent body fat (p = 0.004), visceral fat area (p = 0.0007), total body fat mass (p = 0.018), trunk body fat mass (p = 0.003), waist circumference (p = 0.003), gly‑ cosylated hemoglobin(p = 0.002) at the end of the study compared with baseline. || There were non‑significant reduc‑ tions in Body weight and BMI in the L_K56, H_K56, VL_K56 groups, whereas increases were observed in the placebo and VH_K56 groups compared with baseline values. || In addition, K56 supplementation modulated gut microbiota characteristics and diversity indices in the L‑K56 group. || Conclusions The results suggest that supplementation with different doses of Lacticaseibacillus paracasei K56 has certain effect on reducing body fat and glycosylated hemoglobin, especially at a dose of 109 CFU/day.","Kadeer et al. Nutrition & Metabolism (2023) 20:16 https://doi.org/10.1186/s12986-023-00739-y RESEARCH Effect of different doses of Lacticaseibacillus paracasei K56 on body fat and metabolic parameters in adult individuals with obesity: a pilot study Guzailinuer Kadeer1, Wanrui Fu1, Yaqi He1, Ying Feng1*, Wei‑Hsein Liu2, Wei‑Lian Hung2*, Haotian Feng2 and Wen Zhao2 Abstract Background Studies have shown that probiotics have an effect on reducing body fat on a strain‑specific and dose– response bases. The purpose of this study was to evaluate the effect of a novel probiotic strain Lacticaseibacillus paracasei K56 on body fat and metabolic biomarkers in adult individuals with obesity. Methods 7",ok,,s12986-024-00851-7.pdf,1.000,9,"Over- all, the changes in weight showed a trend (p = 0.06) for significant reduction over 16 weeks, there was also a trend (p = 0.06) for Dose*Time interaction. || We also observed the increase in Ruminococcaceae UCG 004 was associated with decrease in visceral adipose tis - sue (p = 0.04). || Association between changes gut microbiota and measured variables (Table 2) We noted that the change in sTNFR1 was negatively associated with changes in Eubacterium xylanophilum (p < 0.005). || There was reduction in visceral adipose tissue in both the groups (HD W1: 87.08 (39.29) to W16: 79.81 (29.05) vs LD W1: 82.92 (23.72) to W16: 79.16 (21.99)) although not sig - nificantly, the overall change showed a trend for reduc - tion over the time of 16 weeks (p = 0.06). || Amongst these, we observed various gen - era to change during the 16 weeks of supplementation. || Within the low dose Ruminococcaceae UCG 004 increased and Desulfovi - brio decreased from Week 1 to Week 16. || Higher dosage of Sinetrol ® Xpur could also relatively increase the weight loss com - pared to the lower dose in a period of 16 weeks. || Previous studies with citrus flavonoid supplementation in mice have shown increase in relative abundances of various Eubacterium genera [31, 32].","Over- all, the changes in weight showed a trend (p = 0.06) for significant reduction over 16 weeks, there was also a trend (p = 0.06) for Dose*Time interaction. || HDL cholesterol which increased in both the groups was negatively associated with Eubacterium Table 1 Characteristics of the study population during the period of 16 weeks a significant difference within groups, bold p-values indicate significant differences over time Low dose High dose Baseline Week 16 Baseline Week 16 Dose Time Dose*Time n 10 10 9 9 p‑value Age 32.7 (9.6) 28.6 (6.6) Sex (% Male) 40 66.6 Weight (Kg) 83.12 (9.84) 83.18 (11.68) 87.83 (6.09) 84.74 (7.00) 0.46 0.06 0.06 BMI (Kg/m2) 28.67 (0.89) 28.63 || Association between changes gut microbiota and measured variables (Table 2) We noted that the change in sTNFR1 was negatively associated with changes in Eubacterium xylanophilum (p < 0.005). || There was reduction in visceral adipose tissue in both the groups (HD W1: 87.08 (39.29) to W16: 79.81 (29.05) vs LD W1: 82.92 (23.72) to W16: 79.16 (21.99)) although not sig - nificantly, the overall change showed a trend for reduc - tion over the time of 16 weeks (p = 0.06). || Changes in body weight and body fat (Table 1) Body weight in high dose reduced significantly from 87.83 (SD 6.09) Kg at W1 to 84.74 (SD 7.00) Kg in W16, whereas in low dose there were no changes (Week 1: 83.12 (SD 9.84) Kg, Week 16: 83.18 (SD 11.68)) Kg. || Although participants did not change their total energy consumption during the study, carbohydrate % (LD = 8.3 (10.5), HD = 7.3 (12.1)) and fat % (LD = 6.3 (9.2), HD = 6.9 (13.9)) changed significantly over 16 weeks, without differences between the groups (Additional file 1). || The same genera were also associated nega - tively with changes in visceral adipose tissue (p = 0.01). || We also observed the increase in Ruminococcaceae UCG 004 was associated with decrease in visceral adipose tis - sue (p = 0.04).","Previ - ous studies on Sinetrol® Xpur have shown a reduction in body weight (- 3.28 ± 0.24%) and body fat percent - age (- 9.73 ± 0.54%) in comparison to placebo group [19, 20]. || Changes in body weight and body fat (Table 1) Body weight in high dose reduced significantly from 87.83 (SD 6.09) Kg at W1 to 84.74 (SD 7.00) Kg in W16, whereas in low dose there were no changes (Week 1: 83.12 (SD 9.84) Kg, Week 16: 83.18 (SD 11.68)) Kg. || HDL cholesterol which increased in both the groups was negatively associated with Eubacterium Table 1 Characteristics of the study population during the period of 16 weeks a significant difference within groups, bold p-values indicate significant differences over time Low dose High dose Baseline Week 16 Baseline Week 16 Dose Time Dose*Time n 10 10 9 9 p‑value Age 32.7 (9.6) 28.6 (6.6) Sex (% Male) 40 66.6 Weight (Kg) 83.12 (9.84) 83.18 (11.68) 87.83 (6.09) 84.74 (7.00) 0.46 0.06 0.06 BMI (Kg/m2) 28.67 (0.89) 28.63 || We also observed the increase in Ruminococcaceae UCG 004 was associated with decrease in visceral adipose tis - sue (p = 0.04). || Over- all, the changes in weight showed a trend (p = 0.06) for significant reduction over 16 weeks, there was also a trend (p = 0.06) for Dose*Time interaction. || Association between changes gut microbiota and measured variables (Table 2) We noted that the change in sTNFR1 was negatively associated with changes in Eubacterium xylanophilum (p < 0.005). || There was reduction in visceral adipose tissue in both the groups (HD W1: 87.08 (39.29) to W16: 79.81 (29.05) vs LD W1: 82.92 (23.72) to W16: 79.16 (21.99)) although not sig - nificantly, the overall change showed a trend for reduc - tion over the time of 16 weeks (p = 0.06). || Abdominal fat composition At week 1 and 16, abdominal fat composition was meas - ured using MRI on vertebral endplate of L4/L5 with slice thickness of 2.5 mm.","HDL cholesterol which increased in both the groups was negatively associated with Eubacterium Table 1 Characteristics of the study population during the period of 16 weeks a significant difference within groups, bold p-values indicate significant differences over time Low dose High dose Baseline Week 16 Baseline Week 16 Dose Time Dose*Time n 10 10 9 9 p‑value Age 32.7 (9.6) 28.6 (6.6) Sex (% Male) 40 66.6 Weight (Kg) 83.12 (9.84) 83.18 (11.68) 87.83 (6.09) 84.74 (7.00) 0.46 0.06 0.06 BMI (Kg/m2) 28.67 (0.89) 28.63 || HDL cholesterol and LDL cholesterol significantly improved over 16 weeks. || Results We observed HDL, HbA1C, LDL and leptin improved significantly over 16 weeks, irrespective of the dosage. || Conclusions Overall chronic supplementation of Sinetrol® Xpur, irrespective of their dose improved HDL, HbA1c, LDL and leptin and tended to decrease visceral adipose tissue via changes in gut microbiota. || Discussion This study reports that supplementation of the poly - phenol rich extract Sinetrol ® Xpur promotes improve - ment in HDL, HbA1c, reduction in LDL, and leptin which were time dependent and not dose dependent. || Changes in glucose and lipid parameters (Table 1) The fasting glucose levels did not change during the study, however the HbA1c which is a consistent marker of circulating glucose showed a significant reduction in both the doses. || Error t value p-value sTNFR1_W1 Eubacterium xylanophilum group - 83.9 18.4 - 4.5 0.0007 HDL‑Cholesterol Eubacterium coprostanoligenes group - 4.8 1.4 - 3.3 0.005 VAT Eubacterium xylanophilum group - 4.2 1.5 - 2.7 0.017 Body weight Clostridium sensu stricto 1 0.79 0.3 2.4 0.029 HbA1c Flavonifractor 0.03 0.01 2.3 0.039 VAT Ruminococcaceae UCG 004 - 2.5 1.1 - 2.189 0.049 Page 7 of 9 Muralidharan et al. || We also observed the increase in Ruminococcaceae UCG 004 was associated with decrease in visceral adipose tis - sue (p = 0.04).","Association between changes gut microbiota and measured variables (Table 2) We noted that the change in sTNFR1 was negatively associated with changes in Eubacterium xylanophilum (p < 0.005). || We also observed the increase in Ruminococcaceae UCG 004 was associated with decrease in visceral adipose tis - sue (p = 0.04). || Over- all, the changes in weight showed a trend (p = 0.06) for significant reduction over 16 weeks, there was also a trend (p = 0.06) for Dose*Time interaction. || There was reduction in visceral adipose tissue in both the groups (HD W1: 87.08 (39.29) to W16: 79.81 (29.05) vs LD W1: 82.92 (23.72) to W16: 79.16 (21.99)) although not sig - nificantly, the overall change showed a trend for reduc - tion over the time of 16 weeks (p = 0.06). || Other bacterial genera such as Lacto - bacillus and Bifidobacterium have also shown to increase with polyphenol consumption [16]. || The same genera were also associated nega - tively with changes in visceral adipose tissue (p = 0.01). || The HD (1800 mg/d) consumption tends (p = 0.06) to aid weight loss in comparison to the LD (900 mg/d) over a period of 16 weeks. || Nutrition & Metabolism (2024) 21:83 Changes in gut microbiota composition (Fig.","We also observed the increase in Ruminococcaceae UCG 004 was associated with decrease in visceral adipose tis - sue (p = 0.04). || Over- all, the changes in weight showed a trend (p = 0.06) for significant reduction over 16 weeks, there was also a trend (p = 0.06) for Dose*Time interaction. || Association between changes gut microbiota and measured variables (Table 2) We noted that the change in sTNFR1 was negatively associated with changes in Eubacterium xylanophilum (p < 0.005). || There was reduction in visceral adipose tissue in both the groups (HD W1: 87.08 (39.29) to W16: 79.81 (29.05) vs LD W1: 82.92 (23.72) to W16: 79.16 (21.99)) although not sig - nificantly, the overall change showed a trend for reduc - tion over the time of 16 weeks (p = 0.06). || Efficacy and safety of Sinetrol‑XPur on weight and body fat reduction in overweight or obese adults: a 12‑week, randomized, double‑blind, parallel, placebo‑ controlled trial.","week 1 || difference in their bioavailability and bioactivity [8 || increase with polyphenol consumption [16 || reduction in body weight (- 3.28 || week and 1 || baseline and week 16 || baseline (T0 || week 16 || change their total energy consumption during the study, carbohydrate % (LD = 8.3 || reduction over 16 || reduction in visceral adipose tissue in both the groups (HD W1 || change showed a trend for r