th /C21 12 weeks, dose of probiotics /C21 1010 CFU/day, probiotics in capsules, sachets or powder and pro- biotics supplementation without concomitant energy restriction. || The four most prominent features of the studies associated with bene ﬁ cial effects of probiotics were duration of at least 12 weeks, a dose of probiotics /C21 1010 CFU/day, supplementation of probiotics in capsules, sachets or powder and supplementation without the association with energy restriction. || Clinical Nutrition 42 (2023) 835 e847 842 beneﬁcial results of probiotics when the interventions were char- acterized by /C21 12 weeks duration, probiotics dose /C21 1010 CFU/day, in capsules, sachets or powder, and without concomitant energy restriction, and the outcome measures included assessment of body adiposity deposits by evaluating body fat mass and waist circumference, instead of body weight and BMI. || Characteristics of the studies Reduction P a Signiﬁcant Non signi ﬁcant Body Weight (kg) Duration of the study (weeks) /C21 12 (n ¼ 18) 9 (50%) 9 (50%) 0.007 <12 (n ¼ 10) 0 (0%) 10 (100%) Dose of probiotics (CFU/day) /C21 1010 (n ¼ 19) 7 (37%) 12 (63%) 0.477 < 1010 (n ¼ 5) 1 (20%) 4 (80%) Vehicle of probiotic Capsule/Powder (n ¼ 15) 7 (47%) 8 (53%) 0.030 Dairy (n ¼ 12) 1 (8%) 11 (92%) Energy restriction Yes (n ¼ 9) 1 (11%) 8 (89%) 0.136 No (n ¼ 18) 7 (39%) 11 (61%) Number of species /C21 2( n ¼ 15) 4 (27%) 11 (73%) || The bene ﬁcial effects of probiotics were more consistent in trials with /C21 12 weeks, probiotics dose /C21 10 10 CFU/day, in capsules, sachets or powder, and without concomitant energy restriction. || Clinical Nutrition 42 (2023) 835 e847 839 Table 1 (continued ) Author Year, Location Population (Design) Baseline characteristics (Probiotic/control) Sample size (PB/C) Duration (weeks:w or days:d) Probiotics Control group Dietary intervention Methods for assessment of body adiposity Main outcomes BMI (Kg/ m 2) Age (years) Sex %F Strains Dose (CFU/ day) Vehicle L. || Author Year, Location Population (Design) Baseline characteristics (Probiotic/control) Sample size (PB/C) Duration (weeks:w or days:d) Probiotics Control group Dietary intervention Methods for assessment of body adiposity Main outcomes BMI (Kg/ m 2) Age (years) Sex %F Strains Dose (CFU/ day) Vehicle Agerholm- Larsen 2000, Denmark [77] Healthy overweight and obese adults (DB,PC) 30.0 ± 2.8/ 30.0 ± 3.4 38.6 ± 8.4/ 39.4 ± 7.7 75/ 64 16/14 8 w S.","P < 0.0001) and body adiposity in individuals with overweight and obesity [25], but the results of the different studies were controversial. || 95% CI: /C0 0.30, /C0 0.13, p < 0.001), and waist circumference (/C0 0.60 cm; || As main results we observed that ~30% of the RCTs reported a signi ﬁcant decrease in body weight and body mass index (BMI) and ~50% found a signi ﬁcant reduction in waist circumference and total fat mass. || Altered gut microbiota sup- presses fasting induced adipose factor (FIAF) expression, which, in addition to its inhibitory effects on lipoprotein lipase, is a potent regulator of fatty acid oxidation, resulting in increased deposition of triglycerides in adipose tissue [ 15,67,68]. || Considering that the study design of the 33 RCTs presents several differences and that these differences may interfere in the results of the studies, we conducted statistical analyses (chi square) stratifying the studies according to some features (duration, daily dose of pro- biotics, vehicle of probiotics, association with energy restriction, and number of probiotic species) ( Table 2 ). || Characteristics of the studies Reduction P a Signiﬁcant Non signi ﬁcant Body Weight (kg) Duration of the study (weeks) /C21 12 (n ¼ 18) 9 (50%) 9 (50%) 0.007 <12 (n ¼ 10) 0 (0%) 10 (100%) Dose of probiotics (CFU/day) /C21 1010 (n ¼ 19) 7 (37%) 12 (63%) 0.477 < 1010 (n ¼ 5) 1 (20%) 4 (80%) Vehicle of probiotic Capsule/Powder (n ¼ 15) 7 (47%) 8 (53%) 0.030 Dairy (n ¼ 12) 1 (8%) 11 (92%) Energy restriction Yes (n ¼ 9) 1 (11%) 8 (89%) 0.136 No (n ¼ 18) 7 (39%) 11 (61%) Number of species /C21 2( n ¼ 15) 4 (27%) 11 (73%) || The candidate mechanisms to explain the potential role of the gut dysbiosis in the etiology of obesity include increased energy har- vesting by the gut microbiota, increased fat storage in the adipose tissue, reduced secretion of anorexigenic hormones, and low-grade inﬂammation induced by increased intestinal permeability ( Fig. || [58] Meijnikman AS, Gerdes VE, Nieuwdorp M, Herrema H.","The outcomes evaluated in the 33 studies included body weight (n ¼ 28), BMI (n ¼ 29), waist circumference (n ¼ 21), waist-to-hip ratio (n ¼ 12), waist-to-height ratio (n ¼ 2), total fat mass (kg) (n ¼ 17), total body fat (%) (n ¼ 15), abdominal fat area (n ¼ 5), subcutaneous fat area (n ¼ 5) and visceral fat area (n ¼ 8). || As main results we observed that ~30% of the RCTs reported a signi ﬁcant decrease in body weight and body mass index (BMI) and ~50% found a signi ﬁcant reduction in waist circumference and total fat mass. || Clinical Nutrition 42 (2023) 835 e847 842 beneﬁcial results of probiotics when the interventions were char- acterized by /C21 12 weeks duration, probiotics dose /C21 1010 CFU/day, in capsules, sachets or powder, and without concomitant energy restriction, and the outcome measures included assessment of body adiposity deposits by evaluating body fat mass and waist circumference, instead of body weight and BMI. || The evidence of probiotics effects on body adiposity may improve and be more consistent in future RCTs which include methodological characteristics such as longer duration, higher dose, non-dairy vehicle, non-concurrent energy restriction and use of more accurate measures of body fat deposits (e.g., body fat mass and waist circumference) instead of body weight and BMI. || We observed that only around one-third of the RCTs that evaluated body weight and BMI reported a signiﬁcant decrease in these parameters, while 50% of RCTs that evaluated waist circumference and total 