16) or an MD alone for 4 mo (control group, n = 18). || An agrarian diet lead to an increase inPrevotella,whereas diets rich in proteins and fats lead to an increase in Bacteroidesand Clostri- diales [46/C048].H e r ew ef o u n dar e d u c t i o ni nBacteroidesin probiotic- treated participants, probably because of a reduction in protein and lipid intakes from T0 to T2. || We then found in probiotic-treated patients an increase in Butyricicoccus often associated with the low-fat diets [51],w h i c h could be beneﬁcial because of its ability to reduce the incidence and severity of in ﬂammation or insulin sensitivity [52].I ts h o u l db e emphasized that from the correlation analysis we have discovered an inverse relationship betweenLachnospiraand the CRP value: Lachno- spira’s h a v eap r o t e c t i v er o l ei ni nﬂammatory condition[53]. || Probiotics, prebiotics, synbiotics and insulin sen- sitivity.","The Bacteroidetes-to-Firmicutes ratio signi ﬁcantly decreased in the intervention group and increased in controls ( P = 0.004). || Results: After 2 mo of probiotic administration the number of bacterial species ( P = 0.01) and the bacterial diversity assessed with the Chao1 index ( P = 0.004) signi ﬁcantly increased; || 3 ) indicated statistically signi ﬁcant differences in several taxa (P < 0.05) between T0 and T2. || in the intervention group waist circumference ( P = 0.012), waist-to-hip ratio ( P = 0.045), and fasting insulin (P = 0.017) also signi ﬁcantly decreased. || The delta values (ﬁnal value minus baseline value of each variable) were not signi ﬁcantly different between groups, with the exception of ALT values (P = 0.02) (data not shown). || Moreover, the a diversity indicated that there were no differences, in terms of complexity (P > 0.05), between the dietary intervention (control versus probi- otic) at baseline as well as across time. || A signiﬁcant increase both in number of bacterial species (P = 0.01) and in bacterial diversity evaluated with the Chao1 index at T2 was observed in the treated participants but not in controls (Table 2). || / Nutrition 74 (2020) 110749 3 Anthropometric, metabolic, and lifestyle characteristics Anthropometric, metabolic, and lifestyle characteristics were not signiﬁcantly different between the two groups at baseline (P > 0.05).","At the end of the study, in the intervention group we found a sig- niﬁcant reduction in body weight, BMI, waist circumference, waist-to- hip ratio, and fasting glucose, insulin, and HOMA-IR values (Table 1). || Secondary outcomes were changes in body weight, BMI, waist circumference, and metabolic parameters after 4 mo of intervention. || The control group had a signi ﬁcant reduction in body weight, BMI, and fasting glucose and HOMA-IR levels and a signiﬁcant increase in vitamin D ( Table 1 ). || Table 1 Comparisons of change from baseline for study endpoints in the two study arms Anthropometric and blood variables Intervention group Control group T0 T4 p T0 T4 P Weight (kg) 81.5 § 10.4 78.8 § 9.9 0.001 75.5 § 7.8 72.4 § 7 0.015 BMI (kg/m2) 31 § 3.3 30.1 § 3.2 0.003 30.1 § 3.2 28.8 § 2.5 0.013 Waist circumference (cm) 97 § 10 94.4 § 9.5 0.012 93.6 § 10.9 90.4 § 6.1 0.13 Hip circumference (cm) 109.7 § 8.1 109.5 § 7 0.87 108.7 § 8.3 107 § 6.8 0.16 Waist/hip ratio 0.88 § 0.09 0.86 § 0.07 0.045 0.86 § 0.07 0.84 || in the intervention group waist circumference ( P = 0.012), waist-to-hip ratio ( P = 0.045), and fasting insulin (P = 0.017) also signi ﬁcantly decreased. || The delta values (ﬁnal value minus baseline value of each variable) were not signi ﬁcantly different between groups, with the exception of ALT values (P = 0.02) (data not shown). || A signiﬁcant increase both in number of bacterial species (P = 0.01) and in bacterial diversity evaluated with the Chao1 index at T2 was observed in the treated participants but not in controls (Table 2). || / Nutrition 74 (2020) 110749 3 Anthropometric, metabolic, and lifestyle characteristics Anthropometric, m