y weight, BMI, waist circumference, waist-to- hip ratio, and fasting glucose, insulin, and HOMA-IR values (Table 1). || The control group had a signi ﬁcant reduction in body weight, BMI, and fasting glucose and HOMA-IR levels and a signiﬁcant increase in vitamin D ( Table 1 ). || We then found in probiotic-treated patients an increase in Butyricicoccus often associated with the low-fat diets [51],w h i c h could be beneﬁcial because of its ability to reduce the incidence and severity of in ﬂammation or insulin sensitivity [52].I ts h o u l db e emphasized that from the correlation analysis we have discovered an inverse relationship betweenLachnospiraand the CRP value: Lachno- spira’s h a v eap r o t e c t i v er o l ei ni nﬂammatory condition[53]. || in the intervention group waist circumference ( P = 0.012), waist-to-hip ratio ( P = 0.045), and fasting insulin (P = 0.017) also signi ﬁcantly decreased.","The Bacteroidetes-to-Firmicutes ratio signi ﬁcantly decreased in the intervention group and increased in controls ( P = 0.004). || The delta values (ﬁnal value minus baseline value of each variable) were not signi ﬁcantly different between groups, with the exception of ALT values (P = 0.02) (data not shown). || A signiﬁcant increase both in number of bacterial species (P = 0.01) and in bacterial diversity evaluated with the Chao1 index at T2 was observed in the treated participants but not in controls (Table 2). || / Nutrition 74 (2020) 110749 3 Anthropometric, metabolic, and lifestyle characteristics Anthropometric, metabolic, and lifestyle characteristics were not signiﬁcantly different between the two groups at baseline (P > 0.05). || median (range), composition of intestinal microbiota at baseline (T0) and after 2-mo of intervention (T2). || Effect of Lactobacillus rhamnosus HN001 and Bi ﬁdobacterium longum BB536 on the healthy gut microbiota composition at phyla and species level: a preliminary study. || / Nutrition 74 (2020) 110749 Biﬁdobacterium longum BB536 