ersity of Turin, Italy c Department of Agricultural, Forest and Food Sciences, University of Turin, Italy d Department of Medical Science, University of Turin, Italy ARTICLE INFO Article History: Received 12 June 2019 Received in revised form 15 November 2019 Accepted 19 January 2020 ABSTRACT Objectives: Breast cancer (BC) is the most di",Lactobacillus rhamnosus HN001; Lactobacillus rhamnosus; Lactobacillus rhamno-; Lactobacillus rhamnosusHN; Lactobacillus gasseri,,,8 wk,n = 16; n = 18; 34 participants; n = 12; n = 14,randomized; open-label; pilot,diet; sachet; supplement; pasta; milk,P = 0.01; P = 0.004; P = 0.012; P = 0.045; P = 0.017; P > 0.05; P = 0.02; P = 0.001; P < 0.05,"increased; decreased in the intervention group and increased in controls ( P = 0; decreased; reduced bacterial biodiversity and an altered expression of bacterial genes, especiall; 35.0 kg/m; changes in the gut microbiota composition after 2 mo of MD plus probiotics versus MD a; changes in body weight, BMI, waist circumference, and metabolic parameters after 4 mo; 1500 kcal; 15%; 18%; 25%; 35%; 45%; 55%; reduced intake of cheese, butter, meat, and potatoes and very low consumption of sugar; 80%; 100%; 50%","Intestinal microbiota analysis was performed at T0 and T2. || Signi ﬁcant reductions of body weight, body mass index, fasting glucose, and homeostasis model assessment of insulin resistance were identi ﬁed at T4 in both groups; || in the intervention group waist circumference ( P = 0.012), waist-to-hip ratio ( P = 0.045), and fasting insulin (P = 0.017) also signi ﬁcantly decreased. || Keywords: Gut microbiota Breast cancer Mediterranean diet Probiotics Body weight management Introduction Breast cancer (BC) is the most common cancer in women worldwide [1]. || Over- weight and obesity are associated with advanced stage and grade of cancer at diagnosis and resistance to local and systemic therapies [7/C09]. || The largest collection of human-colonizing microorganisms is a complex cellular ecosystem localized at the distal gastrointestinal tract (colon), known as intestinal microbiota (IM) [10/C012].","Applied nutritional investigation Gut microbiota composition after diet and probiotics in overweight breast cancer survivors: a randomized open-label pilot intervention trial Marianna Pellegrini M.D. a, Mirko Ippolito M.D. a, Taira Monge R.D. a, Rossella Violi R.D. a, Paola Cappello M.D. b, Ilario Ferrocino Ph.D. c, Luca Simone Cocolin Ph.D. c, Antonella De Francesco M.D. a, Simona Bo Ph.D. d, Concetta Finocchiaro Ph.D. a,* a Department of Clinical Nutrition, Citt /C18a della Salute e della Scienza, Turin, Italy b CeRMS Labortory of Tumor Immunology, University of Turin, Italy c Department of Agricultural, Forest and Food Sciences, University of Turin, Italy d Department of Medical Science, ",ok,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Gut microbiota composition after diet and probiotics in overweight breast cancer survivors_ a randomized open-label pilot interv(科研通-ablesci.com).pdf,1.000,9,"Moreover, the a diversity indicated that there were no differences, in terms of complexity (P > 0.05), between the dietary intervention (control versus probi- otic) at baseline as well as across time. || (Bologna, Italy) provided the probiotic product, each sachet containing 4 £ 109 colony-forming units (CFU) of B. || Intervention Thirty-four female patients were randomly allocated to MD for 4 mo plus 1 sachet/d of probiotics for the ﬁrst 2 mo of the study (intervention group, n = 16) or MD alone for 4 mo (control group, n = 18). || Dietary supplementation with probiotics, such as bacterial strains exerting bene ﬁcial effects on their host, regulates the gut microbiota structure and function through the interaction with the commensal bacteria and the expression of microbial enzymes [28]. || Methods: A total of 34 BC survivors were randomly assigned to an MD for 4 mo plus 1 sachet/d of probiotics (Biﬁdobacterium longum BB536, Lactobacillus rhamnosus HN001) for the ﬁrst 2 mo (intervention group, n = 16) or an MD alone for 4 mo (control group, n = 18). || An agrarian diet lead to an increase inPrevotella,whereas diets rich in proteins and fats lead to an increase in Bacteroidesand Clostri- diales [46/C048].H e r ew ef o u n dar e d u c t i o ni nBacteroidesin probiotic- treated participants, probably because of a reduction in protein and lipid intakes from T0 to T2. || We then found in probiotic-treated patients an increase in Butyricicoccus often associated with the low-fat diets [51],w h i c h could be beneﬁcial because of its ability to reduce the incidence and severity of in ﬂammation or insulin sensitivity [52].I ts h o u l db e emphasized that from the correlation analysis we have discovered an inverse relationship betweenLachnospiraand the CRP value: Lachno- spira’s h a v eap r o t e c t i v er o l ei ni nﬂammatory condition[53]. || Probiotics, prebiotics, synbiotics and insulin sen- sitivity.","The Bacteroidetes-to-Firmicutes ratio signi ﬁcantly decreased in the intervention group and increased in controls ( P = 0.004). || Results: After 2 mo of probiotic administration the number of bacterial species ( P = 0.01) and the bacterial diversity assessed with the Chao1 index ( P = 0.004) signi ﬁcantly increased; || 3 ) indicated statistically signi ﬁcant differences in several taxa (P < 0.05) between T0 and T2. || in the intervention group waist circumference ( P = 0.012), waist-to-hip ratio ( P = 0.045), and fasting insulin (P = 0.017) also signi ﬁcantly decreased. || The delta values (ﬁnal value minus baseline value of each variable) were not signi ﬁcantly different between groups, with the exception of ALT values (P = 0.02) (data not shown). || Moreover, the a diversity indicated that there were no differences, in terms of complexity (P > 0.05), between the dietary intervention (control versus probi- otic) at baseline as well as across time. || A signiﬁcant increase both in number of bacterial species (P = 0.01) and in bacterial diversity evaluated with the Chao1 index at T2 was observed in the treated participants but not in controls (Table 2). || / Nutrition 74 (2020) 110749 3 Anthropometric, metabolic, and lifestyle characteristics Anthropometric, metabolic, and lifestyle characteristics were not signiﬁcantly different between the two groups at baseline (P > 0.05).","At the end of the study, in the intervention group we found a sig- niﬁcant reduction in body weight, BMI, waist circumference, waist-to- hip ratio, and fasting glucose, insulin, and HOMA-IR values (Table 1). || Secondary outcomes were changes in body weight, BMI, waist circumference, and metabolic parameters after 4 mo of intervention. || The control group had a signi ﬁcant reduction in body weight, BMI, and fasting glucose and HOMA-IR levels and a signiﬁcant increase in vitamin D ( Table 1 ). || Table 1 Comparisons of change from baseline for study endpoints in the two study arms Anthropometric and blood variables Intervention group Control group T0 T4 p T0 T4 P Weight (kg) 81.5 § 10.4 78.8 § 9.9 0.001 75.5 § 7.8 72.4 § 7 0.015 BMI (kg/m2) 31 § 3.3 30.1 § 3.2 0.003 30.1 § 3.2 28.8 § 2.5 0.013 Waist circumference (cm) 97 § 10 94.4 § 9.5 0.012 93.6 § 10.9 90.4 § 6.1 0.13 Hip circumference (cm) 109.7 § 8.1 109.5 § 7 0.87 108.7 § 8.3 107 § 6.8 0.16 Waist/hip ratio 0.88 § 0.09 0.86 § 0.07 0.045 0.86 § 0.07 0.84 || in the intervention group waist circumference ( P = 0.012), waist-to-hip ratio ( P = 0.045), and fasting insulin (P = 0.017) also signi ﬁcantly decreased. || The delta values (ﬁnal value minus baseline value of each variable) were not signi ﬁcantly different between groups, with the exception of ALT values (P = 0.02) (data not shown). || A signiﬁcant increase both in number of bacterial species (P = 0.01) and in bacterial diversity evaluated with the Chao1 index at T2 was observed in the treated participants but not in controls (Table 2). || / Nutrition 74 (2020) 110749 3 Anthropometric, metabolic, and lifestyle characteristics Anthropometri