n Bacteroidesand Clostri- diales [46/C048].H e r ew ef o u n dar e d u c t i o ni nBacteroidesin probiotic- treated participants, probably because of a reduction in protein and lipid intakes from T0 to T2. || We then found in probiotic-treated patients an increase in Butyricicoccus often associated with the low-fat diets [51],w h i c h could be beneﬁcial because of its ability to reduce the incidence and severity of in ﬂammation or insulin sensitivity [52].I ts h o u l db e emphasized that from the correlation analysis we have discovered an inverse relationship betweenLachnospiraand the CRP value: Lachno- spira’s h a v eap r o t e c t i v er o l ei ni nﬂammatory condition[53]. || Probiotics, prebiotics, synbiotics and insulin sen- sitivity.","The Bacteroidetes-to-Firmicutes ratio signi ﬁcantly decreased in the intervention group and increased in controls ( P = 0.004). || Results: After 2 mo of probiotic administration the number of bacterial species ( P = 0.01) and the bacterial diversity assessed with the Chao1 index ( P = 0.004) signi ﬁcantly increased; || 3 ) indicated statistically signi ﬁcant differences in several taxa (P < 0.05) between T0 and T2. || in the intervention group waist circumference ( P = 0.012), waist-to-hip ratio ( P = 0.045), and fasting insulin (P = 0.017) also signi ﬁcantly decreased. || The delta values (ﬁnal value minus baseline value of each variable) were not signi ﬁcantly different between groups, with the exception of ALT values (P = 0.02) (data not shown). || Moreover, the a diversity indicated that there were no differences, in terms of complexity (P > 0.05), between the dietary intervention (control versus probi- otic) at baseline as well as across time. || A signiﬁcant increase both in number of bacterial species (P = 0.01) and in bacterial diversity evaluated with the Chao1 index at T2 was observed in the treated participants but not in controls (Table 2). || / Nutrition 74 (2020) 110749 3 Anthropometric, metabolic, and lifestyle 